    "math_academy_id": "q-111314",
    "knowledge_point_id": "f31f7959-b99f-587a-bec3-374e437a2706",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding Distributions of Squared Random Variables: Many-to-One Transformations",
    "problem": "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n\nLet $Y={X}^{4}.$ The distribution of $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $a$ | $b$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $c$ | $0.2$ | $d$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+d?$",
    "worked_solution": "First, let's add a $Y={X}^{4}$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $16$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $16$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we see that $Y\\in {0,1,16}.$ Notice that we now have two columns for both $Y=1$ and $Y=16.$\n\nWe calculate the probabilities associated with each value of $Y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=0) & =0.2 \\\\ P(Y=1) & =0.1+0.1 \\\\ P(Y=16) & =0.1+0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following table.\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n\nTherefore, $a=1,b=16,d=0.6,$ and $a+b+d=17.6.$",
